# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repository's source of truth is LF (it was developed on macOS, and every
# blob in the index is already LF). Without this file a Windows clone with the
# default `core.autocrlf=true` checks the shell scripts out as CRLF, which
# breaks them before they run: `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` fails with
# "bash\r: No such file or directory", and every line ends in a stray $'\r'.
# That takes out all of the azd hooks — so a fresh Windows clone could not
# complete `azd up`.
#
# Pinning eol here makes the checkout independent of each contributor's
# core.autocrlf setting.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF wherever they run: the azd posix hooks, the
# Dockerfile entrypoints, and run-local.sh are all executed by bash.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Windows-side scripts keep CRLF — the native convention, and the only form
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 parses reliably.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.psm1 text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Lockfiles and generated manifests: leave the tooling's own formatting alone.
uv.lock         text eol=lf
*.lock          text eol=lf
package-lock.json text eol=lf

# Binary assets — never touch these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
